%Presentation Themes 
%"Conceptually, a presentation theme dictates for every single detail of a presentation what it looks like. Thus, choosing a particular presentation theme will setup for, say, the numbers in enumeration what color they have, what color their background has, what font is used to render them, whether a circle or ball or rectangle or whatever is drawn behind them, and so forth. Thus, when you choose a presentation theme, your presentation will look the way someone (the creator of the theme) thought that a presentation should look like. Presentation themes typically only choose a particular color theme, font theme, inner theme, and outer theme that go well together." Beamer User Guide sec. 15.1

% Please remember to set the aspect ratio of your beamer presentation to 16:9
% You can do this by using the following command
% \documentclass[aspectratio=169]{beamer} instead of \documentclass{beamer}

% Requirement
\RequirePackage{tikz}

\mode<presentation>

% Settings
\usecolortheme{NordlingLab}
\usefonttheme{NordlingLab}
\useinnertheme{NordlingLab}
\useoutertheme{NordlingLab169}
\setbeamertemplate{background}[NLCC] %Use this background to get CC license logo
\setbeamertemplate{footline}[NLCC] %Use this footline to get CC license URL
\setbeamertemplate{frametitle}[NL] %Default to left-aligned title; use [NLc] for centered

\setbeamertemplate{navigation symbols}{}
\setbeamertemplate{blocks}[rounded][shadow=true]  
\setbeamertemplate{items}[square]
\setbeamertemplate{sections/subsections in toc}[square]

\setbeamersize{text margin left=0cm}
\setbeamersize{text margin right=2.4cm}
\setbeamersize{sidebar width left=2.1cm} %Reserves the banner strip so \textwidth content cannot encroach
\setbeamertemplate{sidebar left}{} %Empty sidebar — the banner image is still drawn by the background template
\setbeamertemplate{sidebar canvas left}{} %Empty canvas — otherwise the default palette fill covers the banner

% Body-safe sidebar switching. \setbeamersize is preamble-only (it calls \geometry),
% so we update \beamer@leftsidebar, \oddsidemargin, and \textwidth directly. These
% are what LaTeX/Beamer read when laying out the next frame.
\makeatletter
\newcommand{\NLnosidebar}{%
  \setlength{\beamer@leftsidebar}{0pt}%
  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{\dimexpr\beamer@leftmargin-1in\relax}%
  \setlength{\textwidth}{\dimexpr\paperwidth-\beamer@leftmargin-\beamer@rightmargin-\beamer@rightsidebar\relax}%
  \hsize=\textwidth\linewidth=\textwidth%
}
\newcommand{\NLsidebar}{%
  \setlength{\beamer@leftsidebar}{2.1cm}%
  \@tempdima=\beamer@leftmargin%
  \advance\@tempdima\beamer@leftsidebar%
  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{\dimexpr\@tempdima-1in\relax}%
  \setlength{\textwidth}{\dimexpr\paperwidth-\@tempdima-\beamer@rightmargin-\beamer@rightsidebar\relax}%
  \hsize=\textwidth\linewidth=\textwidth%
}
\makeatother

\mode<all>